From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:08:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 135/136] feat(refinery): add acceptance-check.sh packet and
 record, inert (gcp-s7j4.1)

The refinery merges branches whose tests silently drop or swap a named
acceptance item: every gate passes, because no gate reads the acceptance
criteria. gcp-s7j4 approved a refinery read of the criteria against the
branch's own test hunks. This commit adds the mechanics of that read as a
checked-in pack script, so the refinery never hand-builds a packet or
hand-writes a record (the B1 principle merge-push.sh and merge-batch.sh
follow).

`packet` is read-only. It diffs from the merge-base of origin/<target> and
origin/<branch> to the branch tip, never from the target tip, and prints the
criteria verbatim, which test files the criteria name and whether the diff holds
them, the changed files, the test hunks cut at a line boundary, the note lines
that may declare a departure (from ALL the notes, not a tail window), and the
latest notes. It prints one SKIP line and exits 3 for no-branch, no-ac,
no-change or already-judged, checked in that order. One test-path pattern picks
both the named test files and the hunks shown, so the two cannot drift.

`record` writes the verdict the reader reached: three metadata keys in one
update, then for DECLARED and MISS a note, a label and one nudge down the chain
slung_by, parent assignee, mayor. A failed write skips the writes after it and
exits 2; a failed nudge is recorded, not an error. It never writes status,
assignee, rework or routing fields and never closes a bead. Every record exit
ends with a RESULT line.

The script sources merge-push.sh with MERGE_PUSH_SOURCE_ONLY=1 for
resolve_config, as merge-batch.sh does, and reads beads only through gc bd show.
It is additive and inert: no formula step calls it until gcp-s7j4.2, and
--mode accepts only warn until reject mode (gcp-s7j4.4).

Validation: gastown/tests/test_refinery_acceptance_check.sh (next commit), and
shellcheck on both files.

Where DESIGN section 3 is silent: a named path that matches several changed
paths lists all of them; a failed label write stops the nudge like any earlier
write failure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
